{:edb/db-before-t 1794 :edb/db-after-t 1795 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535107 50 #inst "2026-10-08T17:01:43.805Z" 13194139535107 true] [17592186216528 1000 #edb/ref 17592186331576 13194139535107 true] [17592186216528 1104 #edb/ref 17592186331576 13194139535107 true] [17592186216529 1000 #edb/ref 17592186331575 13194139535107 true] [17592186216529 1104 #edb/ref 17592186331575 13194139535107 true] [17592186216530 1000 #edb/ref 17592186331574 13194139535107 true] [17592186216530 1104 #edb/ref 17592186331574 13194139535107 true] [17592186216937 1100 "Which of the following equations is a Riccati differential equation?\n\n- $y' = 4xy^2 - 3y + 1$\n\n- $y' - 2y = (x^2 + 5)y^2 + \\sin(x)$\n\n- $y' = e^xy^3 + y - x$" 13194139535107 true] [17592186216937 1100 "Which of the following equations is a Riccati differential equation?\n\n1. $y' = 4xy^2 - 3y + 1$\n2. $y' - 2y = (x^2 + 5)y^2 + \\sin(x)$\n3. $y' = e^xy^3 + y - x$" 13194139535107 false] [17592186216937 1145 "A first-order differential equation is a Riccati differential equation if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\ny' = a(x)y^2 + b(x)y + c(x)\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Riccati differential equation with\n$a(x) = 4x, \\qquad b(x)=-3, \\qquad c(x)=1$.\n- Equation II is a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n$y' = (x^2 + 5)y^2 + \\sin(x) + 2y$.\nThis is a Riccati differential equation with\n$a(x) = x^2 + 5, \\qquad b(x)=2, \\qquad c(x)=\\sin(x)$.\n- Equation III is not a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n$y' = e^xy^3 + y - x$.\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation since it includes a $y^3$ term.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535107 false] [17592186216937 1145 "A first-order differential equation is a Riccati differential equation if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\ny' = a(x)y^2 + b(x)y + c(x).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Riccati differential equation with\n\n$$\na(x) = 4x, \\qquad b(x)=-3, \\qquad c(x)=1.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\ny' = (x^2 + 5)y^2 + \\sin(x) + 2y.\n$$\n\nThis is a Riccati differential equation with\n\n$$\na(x) = x^2 + 5, \\qquad b(x)=2, \\qquad c(x)=\\sin(x).\n$$\n\n- Equation III is not a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\ny' = e^xy^3 + y - x.\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation since it includes a $y^3$ term.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535107 true] [17592186216938 1100 "The function $y_0(x) = 1$ is a particular solution of the Riccati differential equation\n\n$$\ny' = 3y^2 - 7y + 4.\n$$\n\nExpress this equation in the Bernoulli form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)z = Q(x)z^n\n$$\n\nand state the functions $P(x)$ and $Q(x),$ and the value of $n.$" 13194139535107 true] [17592186216938 1100 "The function $y_0(x) = 1$ is a particular solution of the Riccati differential equation\n$y' = 3y^2 - 7y + 4$.\nExpress this equation in the Bernoulli form\n$\\frac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)z = Q(x)z^n$\nand state the functions $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$, and the value of $n$." 13194139535107 false] [17592186216938 1145 "A Riccati differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny' = a(x)y^2 + b(x)y + c(x)\n$$\n\nTo reduce the given Riccati equation to a Bernoulli equation, we use the substitution\n\n$$\ny(x) = z(x) + y_0(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $y_0(x)$ is a known particular solution.\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\ny'(x) = z'(x) + y_0'(x)\n$$\n\nIn our case, $y_0(x) = 1$. Since $y_0'(x) = 0$, we have\n\n$$\ny = z + 1 \\qquad \\Rightarrow \\qquad y' = z'\n$$\n\nLet's substitute these expressions into our Riccati equation:\n\n$$\nz' = 3(z + 1)^2 - 7(z + 1) + 4\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the right-hand side, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz^{′} &= 3(z + 1)^{2} - 7(z + 1) + 4 \\\\\nz^{′} &= 3z^{2} - z \\\\\nz^{′} + z &= 3z^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a Bernoulli equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d} z}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)z = Q(x)z^n\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x) = 1, \\qquad Q(x) = 3, \\qquad n = 2\n$$" 13194139535107 false] [17592186216938 1145 "A Riccati differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny' = a(x)y^2 + b(x)y + c(x).\n$$\n\nTo reduce the given Riccati equation to a Bernoulli equation, we use the substitution\n\n$$\ny(x) = z(x) + y_0(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $y_0(x)$ is a known particular solution.\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\ny'(x) = z'(x) + y_0'(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $y_0(x) = 1.$ Since $y_0'(x) = 0$, we have\n\n$$\ny = z + 1 \\qquad \\Rightarrow \\qquad y' = z'.\n$$\n\nLet's substitute these expressions into our Riccati equation:\n\n$$\nz' = 3(z + 1)^2 - 7(z + 1) + 4.\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the right-hand side, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{′} & =3(z+1)^{2}-7(z+1)+4 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =3{z}^{2}-z \\\\ {z}^{′}+z & =3{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a Bernoulli equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{ \\textrm{d} z}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)z = Q(x)z^n\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x) = 1, \\qquad \nQ(x) = 3, \\qquad \nn = 2.\n$$" 13194139535107 true] [17592186216939 1100 "Given that $y_0(x) = 4x$ is a particular solution of the Riccati differential equation\n\n$$\ny' = 2x\\,y^2 - 16x^2 y + 32x^3 + 4\n$$\n\nfind the general solution of the equation." 13194139535107 true] [17592186216939 1100 "Given that $y_0(x) = 4x$ is a particular solution of the Riccati differential equation\n\n$y' = 2x\\,y^2 - 16x^2 y + 32x^3 + 4$\n\nfind the general solution of the equation." 13194139535107 false] [17592186216939 1145 "The equation\n\n$$\ny' = 2x\\,y^2 - 16x^2 y + 32x^3 + 4\n$$\n\nis a Riccati differential equation of the form\n\n$$\ny' = a(x)y^2 + b(x)y + c(x)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\na(x) = 2x, \\qquad b(x) = -16x^2, \\qquad c(x) = 32x^3 + 4\n$$\n\nTo reduce the given Riccati equation to a Bernoulli equation, we use the substitution\n\n$$\ny(x) = z(x) + y_0(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $y_0(x)$ is a known particular solution.\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\ny'(x) = z'(x) + y_0'(x)\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\ny_0(x) = 4x\n$$\n\nSince $y_0'(x) = 4$, we have\n\n$$\ny = z + 4x \\qquad \\Rightarrow \\qquad y' = z' + 4\n$$\n\nLet's substitute these expressions into our Riccati equation:\n\n$$\nz' + 4 = 2x\\,(z + 4x)^2 - 16x^2 (z + 4x) + 32x^3 + 4\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the right-hand side, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz^{′} + 4 &= 2x(z^{2} + 8xz + 16x^{2}) - 16x^{2}(z + 4x) + 32x^{3} + 4 \\\\\nz^{′} + 4 &= 2xz^{2} + 4 \\\\\nz^{′} &= 2xz^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve the obtained equation using separation of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dz}{dx} &= 2xz^{2} \\\\\n\\frac{1}{z^{2}}\\frac{dz}{dx} &= 2x \\\\\n∫\\frac{1}{z^{2}}\\frac{dz}{dx}dx &= ∫2xdx \\\\\n∫z^{-2}dz &= ∫2xdx \\\\\n- \\frac{1}{z} &= x^{2} + C \\\\\nz &= -\\frac{1}{x^{2} + C}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nFinally, let's substitute $z = y-4x$ to recover the initial variable $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz &= -\\frac{1}{x^{2} + C} \\\\\ny - 4x &= -\\frac{1}{x^{2} + C} \\\\\ny &= -\\frac{1}{x^{2} + C} + 4x \\\\\n&= 4x - \\frac{1}{x^{2} + C}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535107 false] [17592186216939 1145 "The equation\n\n$$\ny' = 2x\\,y^2 - 16x^2 y + 32x^3 + 4,\n$$\n\nis a Riccati differential equation of the form\n\n$$\ny' = a(x)y^2 + b(x)y + c(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\na(x) = 2x, \\qquad b(x) = -16x^2, \\qquad c(x) = 32x^3 + 4.\n$$\n\nTo reduce the given Riccati equation to a Bernoulli equation, we use the substitution\n\n$$\ny(x) = z(x) + y_0(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $y_0(x)$ is a known particular solution.\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\ny'(x) = z'(x) + y_0'(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $y_0(x) = 4x.$ Since $y_0'(x) = 4$, we have\n\n$$\ny = z + 4x \\qquad \\Rightarrow \\qquad y' = z' + 4.\n$$\n\nLet's substitute these expressions into our Riccati equation:\n\n$$\nz' + 4 = 2x\\,(z + 4x)^2 - 16x^2 (z + 4x) + 32x^3 + 4.\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the right-hand side, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{′}+4 & =2x({z}^{2}+8xz+16{x}^{2})-16{x}^{2}(z+4x)+32{x}^{3}+4 \\\\ {z}^{′}+4 & =2x{z}^{2}+4 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =2x{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve the obtained equation using separation of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =2x{z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =2x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {z}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{z} & ={x}^{2}+C \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+C}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nFinally, let's substitute $z = y-4x$ to recover the initial variable $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+C} \\\\ y-4x & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+C} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+C}+4x \\\\  & =4x-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+C}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535107 true] [17592186217193 1020 #edb/ref 17592186331546 13194139535107 true] [17592186217193 1020 #edb/ref 17592186331553 13194139535107 true] [17592186217193 1020 #edb/ref 17592186331560 13194139535107 true] [17592186217193 1020 #edb/ref 17592186331567 13194139535107 true] [17592186217194 1020 #edb/ref 17592186331577 13194139535107 true] [17592186217194 1020 #edb/ref 17592186331584 13194139535107 true] [17592186217194 1020 #edb/ref 17592186331592 13194139535107 true] [17592186217194 1020 #edb/ref 17592186331600 13194139535107 true] [17592186217195 1020 #edb/ref 17592186331607 13194139535107 true] [17592186217195 1020 #edb/ref 17592186331614 13194139535107 true] [17592186217195 1020 #edb/ref 17592186331621 13194139535107 true] [17592186217195 1020 #edb/ref 17592186331628 13194139535107 true] [17592186217738 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535107 true] [17592186217738 1100 "Which of the following equations is a Riccati differential equation?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={y}^{3}-2xy+4$\n- ${y}^{′}=6x{y}^{2}+3y-x$\n- ${y}^{′}+5y=2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+7$" 13194139535107 true] [17592186217738 1100 "Which of the following equations is a Riccati differential equation?\n\n1. $y^{′} = y^{3} - 2xy + 4$\n2. $y^{′} = 6xy^{2} + 3y - x$\n3. $y^{′} + 5y = 2x^{2}y^{2} + 7$" 13194139535107 false] [17592186217738 1145 "A first-order differential equation is a Riccati differential equation if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is not a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{3}-2xy+4.\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation since it includes a ${y}^{3}$ term.\n\n- Equation II is a Riccati differential equation with\n\n$$\na(x)=6x,\\,b(x)=3,\\,c(x)=-x.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+7-5y.\n$$\n\nThis is a Riccati differential equation with\n\n$$\na(x)=2{x}^{2},\\,b(x)=-5,\\,c(x)=7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535107 true] [17592186217739 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535107 true] [17592186217739 1100 "The function $y_{0}(x) = 1$ is a particular solution of the Riccati differential equation\n$y^{′} = y^{2} - 3y + 2$.\nExpress this equation in the Bernoulli form\n$\\frac{dz}{dx} + P(x)z = Q(x)z^{n}$\nand state the functions $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$, and the value of $n$.$P(x) =$\n{{field-1}}\n$, Q(x) =$\n{{field-2}}\n$, n =$\n{{field-3}}" 13194139535107 false] [17592186217739 1100 "The function ${y}_{0}(x)=1$ is a particular solution of the Riccati differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-3y+2.\n$$\n\nExpress this equation in the Bernoulli form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x){z}^{n}\n$$\n\nand state the functions $P(x)$ and $Q(x),$ and the value of $n.$\n\n$P(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,Q(x)=$\n{{field-2}}\n$,\\,n=$\n{{field-3}}" 13194139535107 true] [17592186217739 1145 "A Riccati differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x).\n$$\n\nTo reduce the given Riccati equation to a Bernoulli equation, we use the substitution\n\n$$\ny(x)=z(x)+{y}_{0}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{0}(x)$ is a known particular solution.\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)={z}^{′}(x)+{y}_{0}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, ${y}_{0}(x)=1.$ Since ${y}_{0}^{′}(x)=0$, we have\n\n$$\ny=z+1\\,⇒\\,{y}^{′}={z}^{′}.\n$$\n\nLet's substitute these expressions into our Riccati equation:\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=(z+1)^{2}-3(z+1)+2.\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the right-hand side, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{′} & =(z+1)^{2}-3(z+1)+2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & ={z}^{2}-z \\\\ {z}^{′}+z & ={z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a Bernoulli equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x){z}^{n}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x)=1,\\,Q(x)=1,\\,n=2.\n$$" 13194139535107 true] [17592186217740 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535107 true] [17592186217740 1100 "Given that $y_{0}(x) = \\frac{2}{x}$ is a particular solution of the Riccati differential equation\n\n$y^{′} = y^{2} - \\frac{4}{x}y + \\frac{2}{x^{2}}$\n\nfind the general solution of the equation." 13194139535107 false] [17592186217740 1100 "Given that ${y}_{0}(x)=\\frac{2}{x}$ is a particular solution of the Riccati differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-\\frac{4}{x}y+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\n \n\nfind the general solution of the equation." 13194139535107 true] [17592186217740 1145 "The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-\\frac{4}{x}y+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nis a Riccati differential equation of the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\na(x)=1,\\,b(x)=-\\frac{4}{x},\\,c(x)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo reduce the given Riccati equation to a Bernoulli equation, we use the substitution\n\n$$\ny(x)=z(x)+{y}_{0}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{0}(x)$ is a known particular solution.\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)={z}^{′}(x)+{y}_{0}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, ${y}_{0}(x)=\\frac{2}{x}.$ Since ${y}_{0}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}$, we have\n\n$$\ny=z+\\frac{2}{x}\\,⇒\\,{y}^{′}={z}^{′}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nLet's substitute these expressions into our Riccati equation:\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}=(z+\\frac{2}{x})^{2}-\\frac{4}{x}(z+\\frac{2}{x})+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the right-hand side, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{′}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} & =({z}^{2}+\\frac{4z}{x}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})-(\\frac{4z}{x}+\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}})+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {z}^{′}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} & ={z}^{2}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {z}^{′} & ={z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve the obtained equation using separation of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {z}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{z} & =x+C \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{x+C}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nFinally, let's substitute $z=y-\\frac{2}{x}$ to recover the initial variable $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =-\\frac{1}{x+C} \\\\ y-\\frac{2}{x} & =-\\frac{1}{x+C} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{x+C}+\\frac{2}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{1}{x+C}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535107 true] [17592186331546 1017 #edb/ref 17592186331547 13194139535107 true] [17592186331546 1089 "q-333311" 13194139535107 true] [17592186331546 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535107 true] [17592186331546 1100 "Which of the following equations is a Riccati differential equation?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=({x}^{2}+1){y}^{2}-3y+x$\n- ${y}^{′}=x{y}^{3}+2y-1$\n- $y{y}^{′}={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+5$" 13194139535107 true] [17592186331546 1113 #uuid "94127b29-55de-5199-8276-7f5854f6b819" 13194139535107 true] [17592186331546 1145 "A first-order differential equation is a Riccati differential equation if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Riccati differential equation with \n\n$$\na(x)={x}^{2}+1,\\,b(x)=-3,\\,c(x)=x.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is not a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x{y}^{3}+2y-1\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation since it includes a ${y}^{3}$ term.\n\n- Equation III is not a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+\\frac{5}{y}.\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation because it contains a $\\frac{5}{y}$ term instead of a combination involving only ${y}^{2}$, $y$, and a function of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535107 true] [17592186331547 1000 #edb/ref 17592186331548 13194139535107 true] [17592186331547 1000 #edb/ref 17592186331549 13194139535107 true] [17592186331547 1000 #edb/ref 17592186331550 13194139535107 true] [17592186331547 1000 #edb/ref 17592186331551 13194139535107 true] [17592186331547 1000 #edb/ref 17592186331552 13194139535107 true] [17592186331547 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535107 true] [17592186331547 1041 "selection" 13194139535107 true] [17592186331547 1104 #edb/ref 17592186331549 13194139535107 true] [17592186331547 1110 #uuid "7ac5fd01-0245-55d3-8361-d40f715b1bbe" 13194139535107 true] [17592186331548 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535107 true] [17592186331548 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535107 true] [17592186331548 1111 #uuid "accd7c10-6320-5282-b2dc-ae7e902b3d80" 13194139535107 true] [17592186331549 1034 "I only" 13194139535107 true] [17592186331549 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535107 true] [17592186331549 1111 #uuid "dd2eb207-452f-58b3-9db6-d9ccedd71138" 13194139535107 true] [17592186331550 1034 "III only" 13194139535107 true] [17592186331550 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535107 true] [17592186331550 1111 #uuid "8bfdab07-078f-5f04-943b-71857df51fae" 13194139535107 true] [17592186331551 1034 "I